=======================
Overview
=======================
This kernel module provides a software architecture designed to verify the
interaction between APC Linux and NOA functionality in a GEM5 fullsoc mode
simulation environment. It simulates common use cases to ensure proper
software and hardware behavior, focusing on efficient and accurate validation
of NOA features.


=======================
Design Considerations
=======================
The architecture is guided by two key principles:

  (1) Abstraction and Reusability:
	Common logic related to network interface verification (Wi-Fi, modem,
	etc.) is extracted from vendor drivers and standardized into a unified
	framework. This promotes flexibility for different technologies.

  (2) Leveraging Existing Code:
	The architecture maximizes the reuse of existing data structures and
	APIs from the existing Linux NOA driver to effectively verify the NOA
	software.


=======================
Architecture
=======================
Based on the existing Linux NOA driver, the architecture is layered as follows:

+--------------------------------------------+
|               Test Framework               |
+--------------------------------------------+
|            Common Network Layer            |
+------------------------+-------------------+
|    Mock Vendor Driver  |  Buffer Manager   |
+------------------------+-------------------+
|       Device Abstraction Layer (DAL)       |
+--------------------------------------------+
|          Existing Linux NOA Driver         |
+--------------------------------------------+


=======================
Common Network Layer
=======================
This layer abstracts common behaviors from various vendor drivers and
standardizes them into a unified framework, allowing vendor drivers to focus on
their specific hardware interactions. It handles generic network-related tasks
and provides a consistent interface for the Linux NOA driver, regardless of the
underlying vendor driver, promoting flexibility and reducing development effort
when switching between different network technologies.


=======================
Buffer Manager
=======================
Buffer manager pre-allocates a pool of data buffers and utilizes a linked list
to track available buffers, dynamically adding or removing them based on
allocation and release requests. It employs a hash table to accelerate data
buffer lookup by token ID, further improving verification efficiency.


=======================
Mock Vendor Driver
=======================
A simplified vendor driver tailored for NOA verification. It excludes
unnecessary complexities (like PHY layer handshakes and Wi-Fi protocols)
that are not relevant to the core verification goals. Vendor driver should
be integrated with the DAL layer to provide the Linux NOA driver with the
necessary information for further operation.


=======================
Device Abstraction Layer (DAL)
=======================
DAL acts as an intermediary between the Mock vendor driver and the Linux NOA
driver,decoupling them and enhancing software flexibility. It handles
communication and data conversion between the two drivers, avoiding direct
dependencies on their internal data structures or APIs.


